00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.370
He paid $750 in taxes the
year he became president

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:37.570
and the year after and even then

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:38.760
it was only because his accountants

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.420
wanted him to pay something.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.520
- He didn't understand the code,

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:46.070
nor would he have had
the time and the patience

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:47.520
to learn the provisions.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.470
He knew that we would produce

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.450
the lowest possible tax
for him within the law.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:53.950
- He deducted personal expenses

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:56.940
like $70,000 in hairstyling services,

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.530
he paid hundreds of thousands
of dollars to his children

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:03.090
who he was also employing as W2 employees.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.540
And before he became president,

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.850
he was hemorrhaging money,
gobs and gobs of money.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.330
So President Trump pays
basically zero in taxes

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.030
because almost all of his businesses

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:15.580
operate at a huge loss on paper

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:17.790
where he is perpetually
inflating the costs

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.470
and minimizing his income.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.850
President Trump portrays
himself as a populist,

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.760
but he operates more like Bernie Madoff,

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:24.750
a guy who cooked the books

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.430
to make himself look like a genius

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:28.780
until his entire venture collapsed.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.060
But are these activities
actually tax crimes

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.050
that could get President Trump

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.140
in legal trouble or even jail time?

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.260
Or is it just legal and smart
tax avoidance strategies

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:40.470
by a financial pro working
their magic over the tax code?

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:41.970
Well, we finally have a window

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.753
into answering these questions.

00:01:43.753 --> 00:01:46.670
(orchestral music)

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.580
Hey, LegalEagles, it's time
to think like a tax lawyer

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.060
because the President's finances

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.960
and the finances of the Trump Organization

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:56.400
are a web of interlocking companies,

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.870
all of which appear to use

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:01.860
extremely suspicious tax
and financial strategies.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.370
Now, it appears that The New York Times

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.550
has access to the President's
personal tax returns

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.210
going back 20 years as
well as the tax returns

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:10.480
of the different Trump Organization LLCs,

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:11.870
of which there are hundreds of them

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:13.530
which are interrelated to the President,

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.640
because of their past through nature.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.240
We'll probably cover the
liability of this whistleblower

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.080
in a different video, but suffice to say

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.850
the information that they
divulge appears to be accurate.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:24.170
Now, the whistleblowers liability

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:25.210
is an interesting question.

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:26.840
But under the Pentagon Papers case,

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.920
New York Times versus the United States,

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.980
unless The New York Times
suborned the actual leaking

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.450
or the whistle-blowing
and actually facilitated

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:38.840
the actual act of
disclosing the tax returns,

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:39.700
The New York Times

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:41.950
is almost certainly
free to cover the story

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:44.260
and will probably escape
any kind of liability

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.990
regardless of whether the person

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.210
who actually blew the whistle

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:52.520
and or leaked the information
has personal liability,

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:53.620
which they might.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:54.810
So the question for the public

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.130
is can we come to any legal conclusions

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:59.310
based on the information
that was divulged?

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.080
Well, let's start with the tax code.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.300
Title 26, Section 7201 of the tax code

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.580
creates the federal crime of tax evasion,

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.340
which has historically been
the main tax revenue offense.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:12.490
There are two potential
offenses under Section 7201.

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:14.750
Subsection 1: The willful attempt to evade

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.680
or defeat the assessment of a tax.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.160
And two: The willful
attempt to evade or defeat

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:20.670
the payment of a tax.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.740
The most common attempt
to evade or defeat tax

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.400
is the affirmative act
of filing a false return

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.740
that omits income and or claims
deductions for the taxpayer

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.270
that they're not entitled to.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:33.090
The tax reported on the
return is falsely understated

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:34.750
and creates some deficiency

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:37.310
and when someone underreports their income

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:39.590
this is an attempt to evade or defeat tax

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:42.130
by evading the correct
assessment of that tax.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:43.040
To prove these crimes,

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.150
the government must prove
that the defendant engaged

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:46.330
in some affirmative conduct

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:48.410
that was designed to mislead the IRS

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:51.410
or conceal the tax liability or assets.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.930
And often this comes in
the form of deductions

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:55.710
that you're not actually allowed to take

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:59.330
or personal expenses that you
say are a business expense.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:00.490
And when there is a pattern

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:02.410
of overstating or inflating deductions,

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:04.620
the IRS can charge you with tax evasion.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.630
And this may be a problem
area for the president

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:07.980
because for years, President Trump

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.730
has classified most of the money he spends

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.550
on his lavish lifestyle
as a business expense.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:14.030
For example, President Trump claimed

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:15.940
the cost of air travel and car services

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:18.360
to and from his private
homes as a write off,

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:21.290
he itemized over $70,000
in hairstyling fees

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:23.450
for whatever is on his head.

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:24.700
He also deduct the expenses

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.520
for Ivanka's his hair and makeup artists

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.900
to the tune of nearly $100,000.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:29.830
Hair and makeup deductions

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:31.190
are pretty straightforward deductions.

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:32.800
A haircut or makeup application

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.470
isn't deductible unless the use

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:37.250
is limited exclusively to the
business appearance itself.

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:39.930
And that's hard with the haircut
because the haircut lasts

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.280
after you have done the
thing that you're doing.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:43.250
And then there's things like using

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:45.790
an investment property for
your own personal purposes.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:47.070
President Trump took a deduction

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:50.200
for an investment property
located in Bedford, New York.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.700
This home which the Trump
family called Seven Springs

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:55.330
has been in the Trump family since 1996.

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:57.040
And the Trump offspring have bragged

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.460
about spending summers
at the 200 acre estate.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:02.007
According to the the Trump
Organization website,

00:05:02.007 --> 00:05:04.190
"Today Seven Springs is used as a retreat

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:05.450
for the Trump family."

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:07.470
If it's a personal retreat
for the Trump family,

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.410
how can it be an investment property?

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:11.210
As an investment property,
Trump has been able

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:14.900
to write off $2.2 million in
property taxes since 2014.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:16.410
We don't have any evidence
that Seven Springs

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:18.230
is actually being rented out or developed,

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:20.220
which is typically how you
would show that a property

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:22.330
is an investment property
rather than a residence.

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:23.910
Instead, the property is described

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:26.150
by the Trump Organization
website in loving terms

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:28.240
complete with a photo spread,
emphasizing its opulence

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.050
and Trump's own tax declarations

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:31.950
also work against this
claim of Seven Springs

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:33.290
as an investment property.

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:34.940
One year after he made the designation

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:36.650
he claimed a tax deduction on the property

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:37.870
for a charitable easement.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:39.490
A charitable or conservation easement

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:41.060
is another way of creating a tax shelter.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:42.140
But basically you can give

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:43.780
the government or your
public interest rights

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:44.613
to your property

00:05:44.613 --> 00:05:45.870
such as the right to prohibit development

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:48.890
or a property to preserve
the nature surrounding it.

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:50.970
That charitable easement on Seven Springs

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:52.530
precluded from being developed.

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:53.363
And then there's things

00:05:53.363 --> 00:05:55.830
like potentially bogus consulting fees.

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:57.940
Companies are allowed to
deduct consulting fees

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:00.180
from their income because
they are a business expense.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:03.410
This reduces the tax bill
because the income that is taxed

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:05.610
is lowered by the amount
of the consulting fees,

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:07.520
just like any other business costs.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:10.140
So if Trump's businesses deducted
legitimate consulting fees

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:12.190
that is not by itself tax evasion.

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:13.340
However, the Trump Organization

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:16.160
consistently reports that
about 20% of its total income

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:17.460
was paid to consultants,

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:19.330
and this applies across all projects.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:21.640
So when Trump made $5 million in income

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:23.850
on a hotel deal in Azerbaijan,

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:26.880
he reported $1.1 million
in consulting fees.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.340
In Dubai, he made $3
million in income on a deal

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:32.590
but reported $630,000 in consulting fees.

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:35.890
These fees, reduce income
and thus reduce income tax.

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:38.110
But on deals where you
don't own the property,

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:40.520
and you're only making money
on the licensing of your brand,

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:42.720
as Trump was doing with
these foreign deals,

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.790
the last thing you want is
a huge 20% consulting fee,

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:47.260
unless you're also benefiting

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:49.210
from that consulting fee personally.

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.760
It's also suspicious that the
fee is approximately the same

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:52.957
regardless of the size of the deal

00:06:52.957 --> 00:06:55.820
and the relative lack
of need for consultants,

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:57.450
but further inquiry would be needed

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.090
to get to the bottom of this.

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:01.710
And let's put aside the problematic nature

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:03.670
of a president getting foreign income

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:05.840
from effectively dictatorships,

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:07.570
because there's even
more damning information

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:10.470
involving consulting fees
linked to Ivanka Trump.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:13.140
Ivanka has a formal position
at the Trump Organization

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:14.800
for which she gets a salary.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:15.890
But at the same time,

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:20.470
it appears that Trump's company
once paid $747,000 in fees

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:21.780
to an unnamed consultant

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:24.600
for hotel projects in
Hawaii and Vancouver.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.230
Coincidentally, when Ivanka Trump

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:29.080
later filed public
disclosure forms in 2017,

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:31.620
she listed payment for
the identical amount.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:32.940
So it looks like Ivanka,

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:34.730
an employee of the Trump Organization

00:07:34.730 --> 00:07:37.300
also got paid a hefty consulting fee

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:39.610
through a consulting
company that she co owned.

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:42.000
Would this raise a red flag with the IRS?

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:44.410
Absolutely, if Ivanka
received a consulting fee

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:46.080
on the same project that she managed

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.250
as an employee of the Trump Organization,

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:50.200
which is what The Times alleges,

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:51.680
this looks like evidence of fraud.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:54.290
Anytime a company pays a
family member consulting fees

00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:56.860
it looks like the family is
conspiring to hide income.

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:58.240
Consulting fees being paid

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:00.030
to family members also looks like gifts.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:03.010
which aren't deductible and in
fact, if the fees were gifts,

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:05.630
then Trump would have
liability for paying gift tax.

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:08.160
The bottom line is that paying
a family member employee

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:11.520
through a shell corporation
could be a way to avoid gift tax

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.610
and a way to avoid payroll taxes.

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:14.910
And it doesn't appear that Ivanka

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:16.490
is the only adult Trump's child

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:18.550
who engaged in shady practices.

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:20.960
That brings us to legal
fees for Donald Jr.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:23.140
The IRS says that legal
fees can be deducted

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.560
when they are directly related
to operating your business,

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.540
but this does not include legal fees paid

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:29.700
to defend charges that
arise from participation

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:31.000
in a political campaign.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:32.090
However, the tax records show

00:08:32.090 --> 00:08:34.550
that the Trump Organization
wrote off business expenses

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:36.420
paid to criminal defense lawyers

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:38.040
who represented Donald Trump Jr.

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:39.590
during the Russia investigation.

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:44.180
The lawyer received at least
$1.9 million in 2017 and 2018.

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:46.740
And Trump wrote off about $260,000

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:48.280
paid to William and Jensen,

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:50.860
a second law firm hired
to represent Trump Jr.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:53.010
As you might recall, a lot
of the Russian investigation

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:54.680
was related to meetings that Donald Jr.

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:57.060
had with people promising
dirt on Hillary Clinton,

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:58.470
so it doesn't appear that this could be

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.510
a legitimate business expense.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:01.970
These are all potential examples

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:03.750
of the most simple form of tax evasion,

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:05.820
deducting expenses as business expenses

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:07.980
that are not actually for your business.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:09.620
It's possible that some
of these deductions

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:12.350
might be defensible, but it
would be an uphill battle.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:14.460
Tax experts often say
that it's very difficult

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:16.920
to surmise tax evasion on
the face of the returns,

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:18.730
and that one usually needs to dig in

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:20.700
to the actual expenses and deductions

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:22.120
before concluding anything.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:24.990
So take The New York Times
analysis with a grain of salt.

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:26.820
But that said, almost
every professional says

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:28.910
that some of the items
in the Trump returns

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:31.540
are so brazen to be
almost certainly improper,

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:34.010
but it's possible for
justifications for them.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:35.520
Of course, this is one of the reasons

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:37.800
why historically presidents
release their taxes

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:40.550
to avoid even the
appearance of impropriety.

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:42.270
And also on the other hand,

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:44.840
these are only the seemingly
improper deductions

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:46.740
on the face of the returns themselves.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:47.640
If you actually dug

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:49.840
into the underlying
expenses and deductions

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:51.330
that appeared to be proper,

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:53.510
you might find things that are even worse.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:55.770
Now, in fairness, these
potentially inflated,

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:57.540
potentially improper deductions

00:09:57.540 --> 00:09:59.770
make Trump's businesses
look less profitable

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:01.180
than they might actually be.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:02.410
And one additional point,

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:05.080
a lot of experts are taking
these returns at face value

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:06.890
since The New York Times is not releasing

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:08.390
the underlying documentation.

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:10.440
But given the improprieties
on the surface,

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:11.273
it's almost certain

00:10:11.273 --> 00:10:13.740
that the underlying numbers
themselves are wrong,

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:15.100
to what extent?

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:16.050
We don't know.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:17.710
Now, a lot of these things look bad,

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:18.600
but it's important to know

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:20.160
that it can be incredibly difficult

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:22.440
to prove the crime of tax evasion.

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:23.700
Because most of these crimes

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.480
require the mental state of willfulness.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:27.670
We talk about mental states
on this channel a lot

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:29.640
because almost every criminal law

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:32.850
requires a certain
mental state, a mens rea,

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:34.730
to prove the crime itself.

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:36.967
And here willfulness is defined as,

00:10:36.967 --> 00:10:40.260
"The voluntary intentional
violation of a known duty."

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:43.030
This is known in the law
as a specific intent crime

00:10:43.030 --> 00:10:45.450
that comes from the case of
Cheek versus United States,

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:48.100
which is a really interesting
Supreme Court case,

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:50.960
where a tax Professor John L. Cheek,

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:54.900
failed to file tax returns and
was convicted of tax evasion.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:57.770
But the court held that
his good faith belief

00:10:57.770 --> 00:10:59.980
that he wasn't violating the tax law

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:01.430
based on a misunderstanding

00:11:01.430 --> 00:11:03.710
caused by the complexity of the tax law,

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:06.400
actually negated the willfulness element.

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:08.520
And that applies even if that belief

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:11.380
is irrational or completely unreasonable.

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:13.110
So this is one of those rare cases

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:15.300
where having a misunderstanding of the law

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:16.930
or sometimes ignorance of the law

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:18.620
actually can be a defense,

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:21.440
which is pretty rare for obvious reasons.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:23.380
But also note that the court has ruled

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:25.070
that an actual belief that the tax law

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:28.100
is invalid or unconstitutional
is not a good faith belief

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:30.270
based on a misunderstanding
of the tax law.

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:31.450
So that's not a defense.

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:34.330
So sorry for all of you
sovereign citizens out there.

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:36.100
And another thing that's
incredibly important

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:37.260
in this context is what

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:40.270
the tax preparer tells the tax filer,

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:42.410
often people use the defense of relying

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:44.830
on their tax professional
for the advice about

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:46.660
what the tax law is, or is not,

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:48.880
which can also provide a defense.

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:51.520
But the bottom line is
this is a subjective test,

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:53.070
a defendant's good faith belief

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:55.310
that he's not violating the tax law,

00:11:55.310 --> 00:11:58.980
no matter how objectively
unreasonable or stupid that is,

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:02.320
can be a defense in some tax prosecutions.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:03.660
And to try and keep my sanity,

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:05.830
I'm going to try to
refrain from putting myself

00:12:05.830 --> 00:12:07.030
in the head of Donald Trump,

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:09.450
and who knows what mental state he had

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:11.870
or did not have when
he was filing his taxes

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:14.750
and claiming all of
these various deductions.

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:16.420
Which brings us to the more complicated

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:18.340
but just as serious potential crimes

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:20.910
under Title 18 regarding a refund.

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:22.830
Tax fraud cases are distinguishable

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:25.460
from those involving false
claims for a tax refund,

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:26.580
which are generally prosecuted

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:28.283
as violations under 18 USC 286,

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:31.040
which is conspiracy to
defraud the government

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:33.910
by obtaining or aiding to
obtain the payment or allowance

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:36.170
of any false fictitious
or fraudulent claim,

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:39.560
and 18 USC 287, making or presenting

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:41.300
false, fictitious, or fraudulent claims

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:43.050
upon or against the government.

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:44.360
The New York Times investigation

00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:46.350
uncovered Trump tax refund

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:48.810
which is currently the
subject of an IRS audit.

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:50.440
And President Trump wasn't lying

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.560
when he said his finances
have been under audit

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:54.390
for an extremely long time.

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:56.410
And this requires a bit of background.

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:58.670
Trump made his reputation
in the 80s and 90s

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:00.860
through lavish properties and casinos.

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:03.270
However, the business has
lost over a billion dollars

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:05.420
and four of them filed for bankruptcy.

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:07.810
In 1995, Trump declared a tax loss

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:09.540
of more than $900 million

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:11.480
related to his Atlantic City casinos,

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:14.010
and the IRS let Trump offset the profits

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:17.140
of his businesses in the future
by using his past losses.

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:19.400
So you can offset $900 million in profit

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:21.270
using $900 in losses.

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:24.090
The tax loss carryovers from
the casinos came in handy

00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:26.740
reducing the Trump
Organization's tax bill to zero

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:28.760
once the company started doing well again.

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:31.440
Now, put a pin in this $900 million loss

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:32.700
because we're gonna come back to that.

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:34.470
But even after the company's recovered

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:36.550
from the casino implosion
it lost big money

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:39.630
on the 15 golf courses that
Trump started buying in 2000.

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:41.160
Trump reported that the golf courses

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:44.210
collectively lost over $315 million.

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:46.370
But were these losses really legitimate?

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:49.330
The Times report says
that from 2016 to 2017,

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:50.840
general and administrative expenses

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:52.470
at the Bedminster Golf Club alone

00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:54.430
had increased by five times.

00:13:54.430 --> 00:13:57.220
Now, why would business
expenses increase five times

00:13:57.220 --> 00:13:58.630
on businesses that were failing

00:13:58.630 --> 00:14:00.350
and never generated a profit?

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:01.760
And well, it's possible that someone

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:03.550
purposefully inflated the expenses.

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:05.270
Inflating expenses on a failing business

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:07.270
is a classic money laundering tactic,

00:14:07.270 --> 00:14:09.870
which you would know if you've
ever seen the show Ozark.

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:11.380
Now, the prospect of money laundering

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:12.810
is beyond the scope of this video,

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:14.860
but I'm betting that we
haven't heard the last of this.

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:17.750
And I recommend checking out
the reporting of Adam Davidson,

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:20.180
who looks at the Trump
properties across the world.

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:22.370
And what he sees is a continual pattern

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:25.180
of failing businesses
needing a cash infusion,

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:26.270
getting a cash infusion

00:14:26.270 --> 00:14:28.410
as a loan from Donald Trump personally,

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:31.260
and then that money basically
disappearing to parts unknown,

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:34.490
which might be money
laundering, we'll have to see.

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:35.580
And not to mention Trump's

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:37.550
incredibly suspicious foreign income.

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:38.930
For example, Trump used to own

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:40.990
the Miss Universe Pageant
which lost millions

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:43.350
in both 2012 and 2014.

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:46.750
But right in the middle in
2013, Trump made millions

00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:49.430
because of a one time payment
from the Agaralov family,

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:52.610
Russian billionaires who have
close ties to Vladimir Putin.

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:54.040
A huge one time payment

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:56.770
in a money losing venture
could be an overpayment,

00:14:56.770 --> 00:14:59.980
or it could be an effort to
funnel money for another reason.

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:02.230
But what we do know is
with these massive losses,

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:04.690
what saved Trump's businesses
from going belly up

00:15:04.690 --> 00:15:06.560
was revenues from The Apprentice,

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:09.050
and a few profits
generated from Trump Tower,

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:10.420
one of the few pieces of real estate

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:11.820
that he actually made a profit on.

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:14.030
And this cash infusion from The Apprentice

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:16.030
was good news for the Trump Organization.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:18.010
But it also meant that
for the first time ever,

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:19.990
President Trump had a big tax bill,

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:23.430
and Trump had to pay $70
million in taxes and he paid it.

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:25.490
But in 2010, Trump told the IRS that,

00:15:25.490 --> 00:15:28.070
oh, by the way back in 2008 and 2009,

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:31.480
his business happened to
lose about $1.4 billion.

00:15:31.480 --> 00:15:33.150
Trump claimed that this huge loss

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:35.690
entitled him to a full
refund of the income taxes

00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:39.200
he had paid in 2005, 2006, and 2007.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:42.440
And amazingly, the IRS quickly
paid out this massive refund

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:45.620
over $70 million, and
state and local governments

00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:48.920
followed suit giving him back
an additional $21 million.

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:51.130
Trump went from telling the
IRS that he was doing well

00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:53.390
to suddenly losing another $1.4 billion.

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:54.720
And where did these heretofore

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:56.530
unknown business losses come from?

00:15:56.530 --> 00:15:59.750
Well, remember that $900
million abandonment loss

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:02.330
he suffered as a result of
the Atlantic City casinos.

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:04.530
In 2009 he reported that he abandoned

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:07.500
his disastrous casinos
which filed for bankruptcy.

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:09.020
And when you abandon a business,

00:16:09.020 --> 00:16:11.240
you can take a deduction
for some of the losses,

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:13.660
as long as you don't
receive anything of value.

00:16:13.660 --> 00:16:15.260
In other words, you
have to truly give it up

00:16:15.260 --> 00:16:17.390
for nothing of value in exchange.

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:19.790
And according to IRS Publication 544,

00:16:19.790 --> 00:16:21.670
sales and other dispositions of assets,

00:16:21.670 --> 00:16:24.150
abandonment losses from
business or investment property

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:26.330
are generally deductible
as ordinary losses,

00:16:26.330 --> 00:16:28.230
meaning you get a one-to-one deduction

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:30.630
on all of the losses that you suffered,

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:32.740
as long as the abandonment is not treated

00:16:32.740 --> 00:16:35.900
as a sale or exchange
of anything of value.

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:38.510
But The Times reports
and public records show

00:16:38.510 --> 00:16:40.870
that Donald Trump actually
did get something of value

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:42.750
in exchange for his old interest

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:45.100
in the bankrupt Atlantic City casinos.

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:47.336
He actually got a 5% stake

00:16:47.336 --> 00:16:49.210
in the casinos successor company.

00:16:49.210 --> 00:16:52.000
So this tax refund of over $70 million

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:53.580
is being audited and has been audited

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:54.950
for basically a decade.

00:16:54.950 --> 00:16:56.250
And tax law actually requires

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:58.730
that all refunds of more than $2 million,

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:01.630
get approval from Congress's
Joint Committee on Taxation.

00:17:01.630 --> 00:17:02.790
And that's where this matter

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:04.800
has been stalled for several years.

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:06.980
We don't know exactly why
this has not been resolved,

00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:09.760
but the word politics almost
certainly comes to mind.

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:11.650
And recall that for over a year,

00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:13.530
we've known an IRS whistleblower

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:15.470
that says a member of
the Treasury Department

00:17:15.470 --> 00:17:16.450
tried to interfere

00:17:16.450 --> 00:17:19.520
with an audit of Donald
Trump or Mike Pence.

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:22.070
It seems that these two
things might be related.

00:17:22.070 --> 00:17:25.370
If Trump loses the audit,
which it appears he should

00:17:25.370 --> 00:17:28.160
he'll be on the hook to repay
not just the 72 million,

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:29.390
but also fees and interest

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:32.900
bringing the total to over
$100 million in repayment,

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:36.010
that alone could bankrupt the
undercapitalized president.

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:38.730
So now we get into the meat
and potatoes of the matter,

00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:40.740
did Donald Trump commit tax fraud?

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:43.680
Tax fraud is generally not
that difficult to understand,

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:45.270
although there are certainly legal ways

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:47.020
to limit your tax exposure.

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:48.280
We'll get to those in a minute,

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:51.220
and arguably, people are perfectly allowed

00:17:51.220 --> 00:17:52.730
to pay the minimum amount in taxes

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:54.720
that they legally have to pay.

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:58.230
There are also clear prohibitions
against lying to the IRS.

00:17:58.230 --> 00:18:00.830
Not that President Trump
has ever been caught lying.

00:18:00.830 --> 00:18:03.100
But let's talk about
making false statements.

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:05.940
Section 7206 of the tax
code deals with tax fraud

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:07.770
and making false statements to the IRS.

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:09.930
These violations typically
result from a taxpayer

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:12.850
falsely inflating deductions
or underreporting income

00:18:12.850 --> 00:18:17.200
on federal income tax returns
to reduce or avoid tax burden.

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:19.790
And a section 7206 felony prosecution

00:18:19.790 --> 00:18:21.210
is not limited to tax returns

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:23.740
it deliberately applies
to, "Any return statement

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:26.890
or other document required
by the Internal Revenue Code

00:18:26.890 --> 00:18:28.460
or applicable regulations."

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:29.780
Though know that convicting someone

00:18:29.780 --> 00:18:32.580
of providing false or fraudulent
information to the IRS

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:34.500
still requires a show in willfulness

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:35.730
which we discussed earlier.

00:18:35.730 --> 00:18:39.300
But it can be easier to prove
then outright tax evasion

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:41.450
in the false information context,

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:42.960
but you still have to show willfulness

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:45.090
and under United States versus Clairborne,

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:47.210
neither a showing of careless disregard

00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:50.730
nor gross negligence and signing
a tax return will suffice.

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:53.560
But it's not all smooth
sailing for the tax filer.

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:55.700
On the other hand,
willfulness can be inferred

00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:57.300
from the rest of the tax documents

00:18:57.300 --> 00:18:59.010
and from circumstantial evidence.

00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:02.130
And proof of a pattern of
falsity supports an inference

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:04.720
of willfulness under United
States versus Conlin.

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:05.640
And while some have argued

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:07.510
that Trump can't form the
requisite mental state,

00:19:07.510 --> 00:19:09.330
because he's so all over the place,

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:11.950
there is a presumption that
you know what's in your taxes

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:13.010
when you sign it.

00:19:13.010 --> 00:19:15.060
And liability for lying to the IRS

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:16.930
is not limited to the taxpayer alone

00:19:16.930 --> 00:19:19.000
and includes people who
prepared the returns,

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:21.530
corporate officers, and
even tax shelter promoters.

00:19:21.530 --> 00:19:23.800
So in the Trump world, many
people could be on the hook

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:26.250
for these returns like
President Trump's accountants.

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:28.650
Allen Weisselberg, CFO
of the Trump Organization

00:19:28.650 --> 00:19:30.310
is probably also on the hot seat.

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:31.510
And during a deposition

00:19:31.510 --> 00:19:33.780
related to a lawsuit
against Trump University,

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:36.090
Weisselberg said only five
people have the authority

00:19:36.090 --> 00:19:37.860
to sign checks for the company,

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:39.890
Donald Trump, Donald Trump Jr.

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:42.600
Eric Trump, Ivanka Trump, and himself.

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:43.470
And you might recall

00:19:43.470 --> 00:19:45.360
that former Trump lawyer, Michael Cohen

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:46.620
also set up a shell company

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:49.160
to conceal the hush money
payments to porn stars

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:51.260
and coordinate strategies
with the National Enquirer,

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:52.530
a process that probably

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:54.120
also involve making false statements,

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:56.520
because spoiler alert,
there isn't a line item

00:19:56.520 --> 00:19:58.720
related to the payment of stormy Daniels.

00:19:58.720 --> 00:20:01.850
So it looks like those
payments tried to be hidden

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:04.420
in normal business operating expenses.

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:06.510
But actual preparation of a false return

00:20:06.510 --> 00:20:08.970
is not necessary to sustain a conviction.

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:10.550
Just supplying false information

00:20:10.550 --> 00:20:12.500
can be enough in some
cases for conviction.

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:14.330
Of particular interest to Donald Trump

00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:15.730
as a real estate magnate

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:18.420
is United States versus
Lefkowitz, where the defendant,

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:20.970
a manager of several real
estate limited partnerships

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:22.030
lied to his accountants

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:23.730
that the projects were pre-leased

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:25.480
to qualified low-income tenants,

00:20:25.480 --> 00:20:27.020
and therefore the limited partnerships

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:28.460
could begin taking tax credits

00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:30.160
when the units were placed in service.

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:32.800
That guy was convicted because
he gave false information

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:33.990
to the limited partnerships

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:36.450
causing them to file fraudulent returns.

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:37.550
But let's take a moment now

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:40.100
to talk about lawful tax avoidance.

00:20:40.100 --> 00:20:42.100
A lot of the President's defenders claim

00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:44.430
that Trump is doing what all
successful businesses do,

00:20:44.430 --> 00:20:47.550
just exploit legal loopholes
to reduce taxation.

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:49.130
And there's a lot of people who argue

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:52.360
I think, persuasively that
no individual or corporation

00:20:52.360 --> 00:20:54.440
has an obligation to pay any more taxes

00:20:54.440 --> 00:20:56.390
than they are legally required to do.

00:20:56.390 --> 00:20:57.340
And tax avoidance

00:20:57.340 --> 00:20:59.940
is just the legitimate minimizing of taxes

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:02.060
and maximizing after tax income

00:21:02.060 --> 00:21:04.090
using methods included in the tax code.

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:06.490
And lots of businesses avoid taxes

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:09.330
by taking all legitimate
deductions and tax credits

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:11.670
and sheltering as much income as possible.

00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:12.778
This is all legal.

00:21:12.778 --> 00:21:15.330
The IRS even has a cheat sheet summarizing

00:21:15.330 --> 00:21:17.760
the difference between
avoidance and tax evasion,

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:19.000
which is illegal.

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:21.110
A tax loophole is tax avoidance.

00:21:21.110 --> 00:21:24.010
A loophole is usually a
deliberate clause in the tax laws

00:21:24.010 --> 00:21:27.540
that legislators created to
reduce certain people's taxes.

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:29.510
And we can argue whether
that's a good idea or not,

00:21:29.510 --> 00:21:31.870
but a lot of the tax code is written

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:33.320
specifically to give benefits

00:21:33.320 --> 00:21:35.590
to certain groups or individuals.

00:21:35.590 --> 00:21:37.820
And since the tax code is so complex,

00:21:37.820 --> 00:21:41.030
savvy tax experts have
found ways to lower taxes

00:21:41.030 --> 00:21:42.810
for their clients without breaking the law

00:21:42.810 --> 00:21:45.580
and taking advantages
of the law as written.

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:46.730
And I have absolutely no doubt

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:49.300
that some of Trump's
deductions were legal,

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:51.470
but the idea that Trump is just a genius

00:21:51.470 --> 00:21:54.220
who was acting like
other business geniuses

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:56.170
should probably be scrutinized.

00:21:56.170 --> 00:21:59.640
So let's talk about the
actually uncontroversial idea

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:04.280
that business losses offset
the taxes on business profit.

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:05.113
If President Trump

00:22:05.113 --> 00:22:07.650
is actually losing lots and lots of money,

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:09.370
and then taking that deduction,

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:11.530
is this really a smart tax maneuver?

00:22:11.530 --> 00:22:13.060
Well, actually, no.

00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:15.070
But during a presidential debate in 2016,

00:22:15.070 --> 00:22:15.970
President Trump said:

00:22:15.970 --> 00:22:17.770
- I have a write off a
lot of its depreciation,

00:22:17.770 --> 00:22:20.530
which is a wonderful
charge, I love depreciation.

00:22:20.530 --> 00:22:22.790
- And that makes sense
because depreciation

00:22:22.790 --> 00:22:25.370
allows the owner to take
depreciation deductions

00:22:25.370 --> 00:22:27.540
on the property that's
probably still gaining value.

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:29.410
For example, if you own a property,

00:22:29.410 --> 00:22:31.010
that's worth a million dollars,

00:22:31.010 --> 00:22:33.260
even if it's appreciating in value,

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:35.810
you're allowed to deduct a certain amount

00:22:35.810 --> 00:22:38.020
of the value of that property per year.

00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:39.220
So if you have a property

00:22:39.220 --> 00:22:41.080
on Fifth Avenue in Manhattan, for example,

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:43.060
it may depreciate for tax purposes,

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:44.940
but it'll keep gaining value.

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:46.160
And this is all basic stuff

00:22:46.160 --> 00:22:47.970
that you learn even playing Monopoly.

00:22:47.970 --> 00:22:50.790
And we can argue over whether
real estate developers

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:52.690
should be allowed to take depreciation

00:22:52.690 --> 00:22:54.920
on an asset that actually
is going to exist

00:22:54.920 --> 00:22:56.270
for a very, very long time

00:22:56.270 --> 00:22:58.070
and is actually appreciating in value,

00:22:58.070 --> 00:22:59.630
but that's actually the law.

00:22:59.630 --> 00:23:01.910
And that's why inheriting
established properties

00:23:01.910 --> 00:23:03.510
in the New York City real estate market

00:23:03.510 --> 00:23:05.570
with no debt attached
like Donald Trump did

00:23:05.570 --> 00:23:07.450
from his father Fred, can be so lucrative.

00:23:07.450 --> 00:23:10.180
You're allowed to take a
whole bunch of deductions

00:23:10.180 --> 00:23:13.620
from the physical property of
the real estate in New York.

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:15.010
However, there's a big distinction

00:23:15.010 --> 00:23:17.410
between a sensible depreciation deduction

00:23:17.410 --> 00:23:19.130
and being forced to take a deduction

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:21.640
because of actual business
losses that you suffered.

00:23:21.640 --> 00:23:23.550
Marty Ginsburg, who was the husband

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:25.230
of Ruth Bader Ginsburg was considered

00:23:25.230 --> 00:23:27.380
a spectacular tax lawyer and professor,

00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:29.077
and he authored a famous article called,

00:23:29.077 --> 00:23:30.750
"The Leaky Tax Shelter,"

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:33.100
where he made the point that
the key to a tax shelter

00:23:33.100 --> 00:23:36.860
is not actually suffering
the losses that you deduct.

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:39.367
Ginsburg joked that a bad
tax shelter idea was saying,

00:23:39.367 --> 00:23:41.750
"Hey, give me a million
dollars of your money

00:23:41.750 --> 00:23:44.280
and run away to it with a
country where you can't get me,

00:23:44.280 --> 00:23:46.730
and then you have a million
dollar theft deduction."

00:23:46.730 --> 00:23:49.270
The point is that your
goal isn't losing money

00:23:49.270 --> 00:23:51.130
than deducting the money that you lost.

00:23:51.130 --> 00:23:54.150
That doesn't make you a genius
or even particularly shrewd.

00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:57.910
Since 2000, Trump lost
more than $315 million

00:23:57.910 --> 00:23:59.810
at golf courses like Trump National Doral

00:23:59.810 --> 00:24:02.780
and The Washington Hotel
which opened in 2016,

00:24:02.780 --> 00:24:05.270
and has lost more than $55 million.

00:24:05.270 --> 00:24:07.790
Across the world his golf
courses are hemorrhaging money,

00:24:07.790 --> 00:24:10.620
the vast majority of the losses
appear to be real losses,

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:13.740
not magic deductions conjured
up by a financial whiz.

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:15.390
In other words, his economic losses

00:24:15.390 --> 00:24:17.280
far outweigh his tax losses.

00:24:17.280 --> 00:24:18.940
Trump's businesses are pretty simple,

00:24:18.940 --> 00:24:20.200
real estate and licensing,

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:22.730
and he appears to be
exceedingly bad at it.

00:24:22.730 --> 00:24:23.700
But as the president

00:24:23.700 --> 00:24:26.550
and his adult offspring
employees like to point out,

00:24:26.550 --> 00:24:28.480
part of the game is to
show bigger tax losses

00:24:28.480 --> 00:24:29.640
than you actually incurred.

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:33.060
So these returns do in fact,
overestimate the losses.

00:24:33.060 --> 00:24:35.770
So to the extent that these
are real economic losses

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:38.690
that offset future income,
this is not financial wizardry,

00:24:38.690 --> 00:24:41.100
this is just going back to square one,

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:43.370
to go back to the Marty Ginsburg point,

00:24:43.370 --> 00:24:44.570
imagine you're at a casino,

00:24:44.570 --> 00:24:46.900
which with Trump that's an actual thing,

00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:50.160
you start playing blackjack
and you lose $1,000,

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:53.540
then you go back to the
table, and you keep playing,

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:55.020
and you actually win $1,000.

00:24:55.020 --> 00:24:58.960
Well, that prior loss of
$1,000 will offset the profit

00:24:58.960 --> 00:25:02.760
of the future income that
you have winning $1,000.

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:05.280
But you're really just
back to where you started.

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:07.900
So that's why you're able
to use the prior loss

00:25:07.900 --> 00:25:11.750
to offset this "gain" that
you incurred by winning.

00:25:11.750 --> 00:25:13.690
And that's essentially
what's happening here.

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:14.910
It's not financial wizardry,

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:17.200
it's just going back to square one.

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:18.840
But it appears from the returns

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:20.370
that Trump's entire business empire

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:22.150
was based on exactly that premise.

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:24.210
He runs up big debts, goes bankrupt,

00:25:24.210 --> 00:25:25.970
and then gets to write
off those huge debts

00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:26.810
for several years,

00:25:26.810 --> 00:25:29.210
then starts the process all over again.

00:25:29.210 --> 00:25:31.740
The Apprentice was really
the first time Donald Trump

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:34.400
ever hit a basis clearing
home run in licensing

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:35.520
and making enough cash

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:37.780
to prop up his other failed businesses.

00:25:37.780 --> 00:25:39.470
But by the time that he was president,

00:25:39.470 --> 00:25:41.030
that money was gone too.

00:25:41.030 --> 00:25:42.370
And that's why people are freaking out

00:25:42.370 --> 00:25:44.630
about the number $421 million,

00:25:44.630 --> 00:25:46.210
because that's how much Donald Trump

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:48.300
personally owes to other creditors.

00:25:48.300 --> 00:25:49.410
And people are worried

00:25:49.410 --> 00:25:50.930
that this is a national security issue

00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:53.200
that the sitting President
of the United States

00:25:53.200 --> 00:25:57.190
owes over $420 million to people unknown,

00:25:57.190 --> 00:25:59.220
including potentially foreign sources

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:00.470
we don't actually know.

00:26:00.470 --> 00:26:03.330
And it appears that most
if not all of those loans

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:05.700
are going to come due in a balloon payment

00:26:05.700 --> 00:26:08.460
that will happen if he's
elected to a second term,

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:10.470
which is completely uncharted territory,

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:12.970
that a sitting president
of the United States

00:26:12.970 --> 00:26:16.310
will have to negotiate with
creditors, domestic and foreign.

00:26:16.310 --> 00:26:17.550
And of course, people remember

00:26:17.550 --> 00:26:20.190
that to avoid exactly
these kinds of conflicts,

00:26:20.190 --> 00:26:22.930
Jimmy Carter had to put his
peanut farm in a blind trust

00:26:22.930 --> 00:26:25.150
to avoid the appearance of impropriety.

00:26:25.150 --> 00:26:27.320
And this also raises the
difference between the fact

00:26:27.320 --> 00:26:30.230
that a lot of corporations in
United States pay no taxes,

00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:32.420
but usually the
individuals, the employees,

00:26:32.420 --> 00:26:34.370
and the people that own those corporations

00:26:34.370 --> 00:26:36.110
do often pay taxes.

00:26:36.110 --> 00:26:39.150
The average tax rate for a
millionaire or billionaire

00:26:39.150 --> 00:26:41.360
in the United States is about 25%,

00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:43.600
which is lower than the average taxes

00:26:43.600 --> 00:26:45.570
that a normal person is going to pay

00:26:45.570 --> 00:26:48.860
but still well above the
zero taxes that Donald Trump

00:26:48.860 --> 00:26:50.800
has paid for most of his career.

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:53.210
And here Donald Trump's
personal tax liabilities

00:26:53.210 --> 00:26:55.420
almost inextricably linked
to the Trump Organization

00:26:55.420 --> 00:26:57.300
because they're passed through entities.

00:26:57.300 --> 00:26:58.280
So there's a big difference

00:26:58.280 --> 00:27:00.190
between a corporation like Amazon

00:27:00.190 --> 00:27:02.200
that gets taxed as a corporation

00:27:02.200 --> 00:27:04.920
and an LLC, which just
the tax is passed through

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:07.050
to the person or people that own it.

00:27:07.050 --> 00:27:08.640
And let's talk about Amazon for a second

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:10.620
because that's an interesting comparison.

00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:13.350
Amazon famously pays little to no taxes.

00:27:13.350 --> 00:27:15.020
And this appears to be legal.

00:27:15.020 --> 00:27:16.210
But this is very different

00:27:16.210 --> 00:27:18.120
from what the Trump Organization does.

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:20.530
Amazon is a business that reinvests

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:23.550
all of its profit back into
the business year after year.

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:26.700
So for years, Jeff Bezos
was focused on making Amazon

00:27:26.700 --> 00:27:30.120
as big as possible, dominating
the online shopping space

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:31.380
to such an extent that the company

00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:33.580
was even able to pressure
brick and mortar retailers

00:27:33.580 --> 00:27:35.420
by undercutting their profits.

00:27:35.420 --> 00:27:37.990
And the company did this
by aggressively investing

00:27:37.990 --> 00:27:39.150
in research and development

00:27:39.150 --> 00:27:40.480
and borrowing more and more money

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:41.970
to grow even bigger.

00:27:41.970 --> 00:27:43.980
If Amazon had kept more of its profits,

00:27:43.980 --> 00:27:45.980
it's reported income would
have been much higher,

00:27:45.980 --> 00:27:47.080
and they would be responsible

00:27:47.080 --> 00:27:50.070
for paying higher taxes on those profits.

00:27:50.070 --> 00:27:51.550
But the Trump Organization appears

00:27:51.550 --> 00:27:53.250
to be a different animal entirely.

00:27:53.250 --> 00:27:55.090
It doesn't have research
and development costs,

00:27:55.090 --> 00:27:57.540
it's not buying more and more assets

00:27:57.540 --> 00:27:59.820
to offset any particular profit.

00:27:59.820 --> 00:28:01.280
And Donald Trump's main place

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:03.740
have always been in real
estate and licensing.

00:28:03.740 --> 00:28:04.990
And it appears that the losses

00:28:04.990 --> 00:28:07.140
that he's reporting on his taxes

00:28:07.140 --> 00:28:09.040
are actual losses that his businesses

00:28:09.040 --> 00:28:11.020
have been suffering year,
after year, after year.

00:28:11.020 --> 00:28:13.780
They're not necessarily paper
losses that are offsetting

00:28:13.780 --> 00:28:15.690
a whole bunch of revenue in other places,

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:18.800
his businesses are
generating loss after loss.

00:28:18.800 --> 00:28:20.720
And in some cases, it's not clear

00:28:20.720 --> 00:28:21.900
where the money is coming from

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:24.290
to prop up these businesses that lose

00:28:24.290 --> 00:28:26.570
hundreds of millions of
dollars year over year.

00:28:26.570 --> 00:28:28.660
And the bottom line is that rich people

00:28:28.660 --> 00:28:32.130
do pay less in taxes in the US.

00:28:32.130 --> 00:28:33.830
But even still, Donald Trump

00:28:33.830 --> 00:28:35.580
should have been paying some taxes

00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:37.770
and all the tax avoidance
strategies in the world

00:28:37.770 --> 00:28:40.620
still don't make this typical situation.

00:28:40.620 --> 00:28:43.530
When asked about the tax returns
at the presidential debate,

00:28:43.530 --> 00:28:46.530
President Trump said that
he'd paid millions in taxes.

00:28:46.530 --> 00:28:49.040
- I paid millions of dollars in taxes,

00:28:49.040 --> 00:28:51.500
millions of dollars of income tax,

00:28:51.500 --> 00:28:54.580
I paid $38 million one year.

00:28:54.580 --> 00:28:57.610
I paid $27 million one year.
- Show us you tax returns.

00:28:57.610 --> 00:28:58.790
- And he might have

00:28:58.790 --> 00:29:01.250
but almost certainly not
federal income taxes.

00:29:01.250 --> 00:29:03.750
Perhaps he's paid property
taxes or payroll taxes

00:29:03.750 --> 00:29:05.240
but not income taxes.

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:07.220
Tax experts say that he
can clear everything up

00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:09.290
by releasing a single page of his 1040

00:29:09.290 --> 00:29:11.860
to show just how much he has paid in taxes

00:29:11.860 --> 00:29:14.060
without divulging more information,

00:29:14.060 --> 00:29:16.080
but he's provided no concrete details

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:18.490
to dispute any part of The
New York Times reporting.

00:29:18.490 --> 00:29:19.323
And the worst issue

00:29:19.323 --> 00:29:21.330
is that many of the President's properties

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:23.160
are being held up artificially with loans.

00:29:23.160 --> 00:29:25.500
For many he hasn't paid a
single dime of principle,

00:29:25.500 --> 00:29:27.990
and hundreds of millions
of dollars in loans

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:29.800
are coming due in the next four years.

00:29:29.800 --> 00:29:31.760
And because the properties are so risky,

00:29:31.760 --> 00:29:33.740
Trump had to personally
guarantee the loans,

00:29:33.740 --> 00:29:35.500
meaning that even if the property fails,

00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:37.860
the creditor can go after Trump himself.

00:29:37.860 --> 00:29:39.910
One might be able to refinance these loans

00:29:39.910 --> 00:29:41.110
but that's extremely difficult

00:29:41.110 --> 00:29:42.160
when the underlying properties

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:44.060
are losing money hand over fist.

00:29:44.060 --> 00:29:45.870
And it's completely uncharted territory

00:29:45.870 --> 00:29:48.450
for a sitting president to
either renegotiate the loans

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:51.350
or have a creditor foreclose
on the sitting president.

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:53.550
Not to mention the obvious
conflicts of interest.

00:29:53.550 --> 00:29:55.160
Nancy Pelosi and other Democrats

00:29:55.160 --> 00:29:56.989
have called this a
national security issue.

00:29:56.989 --> 00:29:58.760
- He has exposure

00:29:58.760 --> 00:30:01.780
to the tune of hundreds
of millions of dollars

00:30:01.780 --> 00:30:05.850
to whom over $400 million in leverage

00:30:05.850 --> 00:30:09.460
that somebody has over the
President of the United States.

00:30:09.460 --> 00:30:11.210
- Some have pointed out that such leverage

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:13.040
often prevents individuals from getting

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:15.620
even a security clearance
for the exact same reason.

00:30:15.620 --> 00:30:17.550
- One of the most important things

00:30:17.550 --> 00:30:21.150
that the FBI and the
background checks look into

00:30:21.150 --> 00:30:24.370
is are you potentially
beholden to anybody,

00:30:24.370 --> 00:30:26.490
or any entity, any country

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:28.340
that could use that leverage

00:30:28.340 --> 00:30:30.350
against the interests of the United States

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:31.910
in our national security,

00:30:31.910 --> 00:30:35.840
in order to extort or exploit
or otherwise manipulate you.

00:30:35.840 --> 00:30:36.750
- Though by the same token,

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:38.960
the total assets that President Trump has

00:30:38.960 --> 00:30:40.990
are probably worth
enough to cover the loans

00:30:40.990 --> 00:30:43.670
if he liquidated all of these properties.

00:30:43.670 --> 00:30:46.280
They are not worth what
Trump says they're worth,

00:30:46.280 --> 00:30:48.450
sometimes he claims $2.5 billion dollars.

00:30:48.450 --> 00:30:50.010
But if you liquidated all the assets,

00:30:50.010 --> 00:30:53.210
it would probably be enough
to cover the $421 million

00:30:53.210 --> 00:30:55.390
that is likely to come
due in balloon payments

00:30:55.390 --> 00:30:56.430
in the next four years.

00:30:56.430 --> 00:30:58.410
But this is surprising only to people

00:30:58.410 --> 00:30:59.680
who haven't been paying attention.

00:30:59.680 --> 00:31:01.640
Recall that the Trump foundation charity

00:31:01.640 --> 00:31:02.900
was shut down for fraud.

00:31:02.900 --> 00:31:05.810
Trump University paid a
settlement of $25 million

00:31:05.810 --> 00:31:07.730
for essentially defrauding students,

00:31:07.730 --> 00:31:09.310
though it admitted no fault.

00:31:09.310 --> 00:31:11.010
And we already knew that President Trump

00:31:11.010 --> 00:31:13.267
owes Deutsche Bank hundreds
of millions of dollars

00:31:13.267 --> 00:31:14.690
and The Times investigation

00:31:14.690 --> 00:31:16.290
reveals that his business enterprises

00:31:16.290 --> 00:31:18.310
are once again on the verge of bankruptcy.

